---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import TemperatureDemo from '../../components/TemperatureDemo.astro';
import { commitUrl, evidence, type ClaimId } from '../../data/claims';

const sources: ClaimId[] = [
  'cf.split', 'cf.val.n', 'cf.test.n', 'cf.valMacroF1', 'cf.val.macroF1Recomputed', 'cf.test.macroF1',
  'cf.val.melRecall', 'cf.test.melRecall', 'cf.val.melPrecision', 'cf.test.melPrecision',
  'cf.test.melFp', 'cf.test.melFpNv', 'cf.latency.explain', 'cf.latency.fast',
  'cf.temperature', 'cf.ece.before', 'cf.ece.after',
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What it does' },
  { id: 'results', label: 'Results' },
  { id: 'silent', label: 'The silent failure' },
  { id: 'calibration', label: 'Calibration' },
  { id: 'latency', label: 'Latency' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Limits' },
  { id: 'reproduce', label: 'Reproduce the test numbers' },
];
const bug = `state = torch.load("models/best_model.pth")   # stale, image-only
model.load_state_dict(state, strict=False)     # mismatched weights skipped, no error`;
const fixed = `result = model.load_state_dict(state, strict=False)
print(f"missing={len(result.missing_keys)} "
      f"unexpected={len(result.unexpected_keys)}")`;
const fix = commitUrl(evidence.cfCollapseFix.repo, evidence.cfCollapseFix.sha);
const loud = commitUrl(evidence.cfSilentLoad.repo, evidence.cfSilentLoad.sha);
const temperature = commitUrl(evidence.cfTemperature.repo, evidence.cfTemperature.sha);
---
<CaseStudy
  title="Cancer Fusion AI"
  dek="A skin lesion classifier that combines dermoscopy images with patient metadata, evaluated as a triage filter."
  description="A multimodal skin lesion classifier on HAM10000, with held-out test metrics, a silent checkpoint failure and its fix, and Grad-CAM latency work."
  year="2026"
  stack={['PyTorch', 'ResNet50', 'Grad-CAM', 'FastAPI', 'React']}
  links={[{ label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/Avnish1505/cancer-fusion-ai' }]}
  sources={sources}
  toc={toc}
  ogImage="/og/cancer-fusion.png"
>
  <h2 id="what">What it does</h2>
  <p>
    The model takes a dermoscopy image plus three pieces of patient metadata: age, sex and lesion location. ResNet50 image
    features and the metadata go through a small MLP head that predicts one of seven lesion types. It is trained on HAM10000,
    about ten thousand images.
  </p>
  <p>
    The split is by <Claim id="cf.split" />, not by image. Some lesions have several photos, and an image-level split would put
    the same lesion on both sides, which inflates validation scores without making the model any better.
  </p>

  <h2 id="results">Results</h2>
  <p>
    The repository reports validation macro-F1, and the validation set is the one used to choose the checkpoint, so that number
    flatters the model a little. I recomputed the metrics on the held-out test set from the logits committed in the repository.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><td></td><th scope="col">Validation</th><th scope="col">Test</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Images</th><td><Claim id="cf.val.n" /></td><td><Claim id="cf.test.n" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Macro-F1</th><td><Claim id="cf.valMacroF1" /></td><td><Claim id="cf.test.macroF1" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Melanoma recall</th><td><Claim id="cf.val.melRecall" /></td><td><Claim id="cf.test.melRecall" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Melanoma precision</th><td><Claim id="cf.val.melPrecision" /></td><td><Claim id="cf.test.melPrecision" /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Recomputing validation macro-F1 from the same logits gives <Claim id="cf.val.macroF1Recomputed" />, matching the value stored
    in the shipped checkpoint, which is how I know the logits came from that model.
  </p>
  <p>
    I report macro-F1 rather than accuracy because benign nevi are about two thirds of the data, and a model can score well on
    accuracy while failing the rare classes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Melanoma precision is low. On the test set the model flags <Claim id="cf.test.melFp" /> images as melanoma that are not, and <Claim id="cf.test.melFpNv" /> of those are benign nevi. A missed melanoma costs more than an unnecessary referral, so that
    trade-off is defensible for a triage filter and not for a diagnosis. The model is not clinically validated and should not
    be used on patients.
  </p>

  <h2 id="silent">The silent failure</h2>
  <p>
    For a while the model predicted the same class for every image, and nothing raised an error. The loader used <code>strict=False</code> and was loading a stale image-only checkpoint into the fusion model. Weights that did not match were
    skipped without a word, and the predictions collapsed.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={bug} label="the shape of the bug" del={[2]} />
  <CodeBlock code={fixed} label="the loud version" add={[2, 3]} />
  <p class="small muted"><span class="illustrative">Illustrative.</span> <code>load_state_dict</code> returns the missing and unexpected keys. With <code>strict=False</code> nobody reads them unless you ask.</p>
  <p>
    The fix had two parts: <a href={fix}>track the correct fusion checkpoint and drop the stale one</a>, and <a href={loud}>make loading print missing and unexpected key counts instead of failing silently</a>. The same commit removed a
    leak in how the metadata scaler was fitted.
  </p>

  <h2 id="calibration">Calibration</h2>
  <p>
    A classifier's softmax output is often more confident than it is right. Temperature scaling divides every logit by one number,
    <em>T</em>, before the softmax. I <a href={temperature}>fitted it on the validation set</a> by minimising negative log-likelihood,
    never on the test set, and got <em>T</em> = <Claim id="cf.temperature" />.
  </p>
  <TemperatureDemo />
  <p>
    On the test set the fitted value is very slightly worse on expected calibration error than an older hand-picked T = 2.5, and better on
    log-likelihood. I kept the fitted one because it was chosen by a rule on data the test set never saw.
  </p>

  <h2 id="latency">Latency</h2>
  <p>
    I profiled inference phase by phase. The backward pass for Grad-CAM dominated each request, so I put explanations behind an
    opt-in flag. When no explanation is needed, local latency drops from <Claim id="cf.latency.explain" /> to <Claim id="cf.latency.fast" /> per request. These are local measurements on one CPU worker, not production numbers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>There is no always-on public demo.</li>
    <li>One dataset from a research collection, so performance on other populations and cameras is unknown.</li>
    <li>The training and validation accuracy figures in the project README come from a past run and cannot be reproduced from the repository. The README says so.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="reproduce">Reproduce the test numbers</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" label="in this site's repository" code={`pip install numpy
python scripts/derive/cancer_fusion_metrics.py`} />
</CaseStudy>
